    q: "Why do some GMT morning hours convert to the previous day in EST?",
    a: "Because Eastern Standard Time is 5 hours behind GMT (and EDT is 4 hours behind). Any GMT time before 5:00 AM GMT in winter (or before 4:00 AM GMT in summer) occurs before midnight on the U.S. East Coast, placing it on the previous calendar day. For example, 2:00 AM GMT on Thursday is 9:00 PM EST on Wednesday evening."
