<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     OFFSET MECHANICS & DST AMBIGUITY
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="method-section reveal" id="offset-mechanics" aria-label="EST vs EDT Offset Mechanics">
  <div class="method-intro">
    <div class="eyebrow">Offset Mechanics &amp; Daylight Saving</div>
    <h2>EST vs EDT: Navigating the Seasonal Transitions</h2>
    <p>
      While users commonly search for "EST" as a shorthand for the entire North American Eastern Time corridor, Eastern Standard Time (EST) strictly refers to the winter offset of <strong>UTC-5</strong>. During the warmer months, the zone advances to Eastern Daylight Time (EDT), which operates at <strong>UTC-4</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Understanding the seasonal mechanics between Greenwich Mean Time (GMT, UTC+0), British Summer Time (BST, UTC+1), Eastern Standard Time (EST, UTC-5), and Eastern Daylight Time (EDT, UTC-4) is essential to avoid transatlantic scheduling errors:
    </p>
    <ul style="margin: 16px 0 0; padding-left: 1.5rem; color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      <li><strong>Standard Time Difference (5 Hours)</strong>: For roughly 48 weeks of the year, London and New York are 5 hours apart. In winter, London observes GMT (UTC+0) while New York observes EST (UTC-5). In summer, London observes BST (UTC+1) while New York observes EDT (UTC-4). Both regions advance by one hour, maintaining the exact same 5-hour gap.</li>
      <li><strong>DST Transition Mismatch (4 Hours)</strong>: The United States and the United Kingdom change their clocks on different dates. During the 2- to 3-week window in March and the 1-week window in late October/early November, the time difference narrows temporarily to <strong>4 hours</strong>. London and New York are <strong>never 6 hours apart</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Spring-Forward Mechanics (2:00 AM to 3:00 AM Skipped)</strong>: When Eastern Time springs forward on the second Sunday of March, local clocks jump directly from 1:59:59 AM EST to 3:00:00 AM EDT. The 2:00 AM–3:00 AM hour is skipped on that day (a 23-hour day).</li>
      <li><strong>Fall-Back Mechanics (1:00 AM to 2:00 AM Repeated)</strong>: When Eastern Time falls back on the first Sunday of November, local clocks reach 1:59:59 AM EDT and turn back to 1:00:00 AM EST. The entire 1:00 AM–2:00 AM interval is repeated twice (a 25-hour day). Automated cron jobs and databases must account for this repeated hour to avoid duplicate executions.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="method-grid">
    <div class="method-card">
      <h3>Standard Offset (EST)</h3>
      <code>GMT - 5 Hours</code>
      <p>Active November to March. UTC-5 standard offset across New York, Toronto, and Washington, D.C.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="method-card">
      <h3>Daylight Saving (EDT)</h3>
      <code>GMT - 4 Hours</code>
      <p>Active March to November. UTC-4 daylight offset, shifting clocks forward for longer evening light.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="method-card">
      <h3>DST Mismatch Windows</h3>
      <code>4-Hour Gap (Spring &amp; Fall)</code>
      <p>US and UK clock changes occur weeks apart, temporarily reducing the transatlantic difference to 4 hours.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Verified 2026 & 2027 DST Schedule Table -->
  <div style="margin-top: 32px;">
