  <!-- FOREX TRADING OVERLAP -->
  <div style="margin-top: 36px; padding: 24px; background: rgba(18, 69, 93, 0.4); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 8px;">
    <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--accent);">London–New York Forex Trading Overlap</h3>
    <p>
      In international financial markets, the overlap between the London and New York trading sessions represents the most active, high-volume foreign exchange period of the global trading day. Unlike general 9-to-5 corporate working hours, the interbank Forex session overlap is measured from the opening of North American institutional desks:
    </p>
    <ul style="margin: 12px 0; padding-left: 1.5rem; color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      <li><strong>Eastern Local Window</strong>: <strong>8:00 AM – 12:00 PM ET</strong> (4-hour duration).</li>
      <li><strong>Winter UTC/GMT Equivalent</strong>: Approximately <strong>1:00 PM – 5:00 PM GMT</strong> (when New York is on EST and London is on GMT).</li>
      <li><strong>Summer UTC/GMT Equivalent</strong>: Approximately <strong>12:00 PM – 4:00 PM GMT</strong> (corresponding to <strong>1:00 PM – 5:00 PM BST</strong> in London and 8:00 AM – 12:00 PM EDT in New York).</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
      During this 4-hour window, institutional banks, hedge funds, and currency dealers across Western Europe and North America operate concurrently. This convergence generates peak global liquidity, narrow bid-ask spreads, and the heaviest daily trading volumes for transatlantic pairs such as GBP/USD, EUR/USD, and USD/CAD.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

