<section id="educational-topics" class="reveal" aria-label="History of Greenwich Mean Time and Eastern Time">
  <h2>The History of Greenwich Mean Time &amp; Eastern Time</h2>
  <div class="content">
    <article>
      <h3>History of Greenwich Mean Time</h3>
      <p>
        Greenwich Mean Time (GMT) was originally established in 1675 when King Charles II founded the Royal Observatory in Greenwich, London, to help mariners calculate longitude at sea.
      </p>
      <p>
        In 1884, the International Meridian Conference selected the Greenwich meridian as the official prime meridian (0° longitude) for global navigation and timekeeping. This established GMT as the primary solar time standard for the world.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>History of the Eastern Time Zone</h3>
      <p>
        Before standard time zones were established, municipalities in North America kept local solar time, leading to scheduling confusion on railroad networks. In 1883, railroads coordinated to establish four regional time zones.
      </p>
      <p>
        The U.S. Congress formally codified these zones in the Standard Time Act of 1918, which officially defined Eastern Standard Time (EST) and established national daylight saving rules.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <h2>Why GMT and UTC Still Matter in a Digital World</h2>
  <div class="content">
    <article>
      <h3>Coordinated Universal Time (UTC)</h3>
      <p>
        In 1972, Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) replaced GMT as the scientific atomic time standard. While GMT is based on the Earth's rotation, UTC is measured by highly precise atomic clocks.
      </p>
      <p>
        For general scheduling, GMT and UTC are practically identical, but UTC is the standard used by modern operating systems, server logs, and software development frameworks. Read our full comparison on <a href="/gmt-vs-utc/">GMT vs UTC</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>DevOps, Cloud Deployments, and Support Shifts</h3>
      <p>
        SaaS companies routinely coordinate database migrations and software releases using GMT/UTC timestamps to avoid downtime during business hours.
      </p>
      <p>
        Customer support teams use timezone conversions to manage shift handovers between UK, US, and Asian desks, ensuring 24/7 service coverage.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

