<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     COMMON CONFUSIONS
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="common-confusions" class="source-notes reveal" aria-label="Common Timezone Confusions Explained">
  <h2>Common Confusions: GMT, UTC, EST, and EDT</h2>
  <p>
    Timezone terminology can be confusing. Let's clarify the key definitions and differences:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>GMT vs. UTC</strong>: GMT is a solar time standard based on the prime meridian in Greenwich. UTC is the modern atomic time standard. While they operate at the same offset (+0) for general purposes, UTC is the standard used in computer science and network protocols. Read our detailed comparison on <a href="/gmt-vs-utc/">GMT vs UTC</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>EST vs. ET</strong>: Eastern Standard Time (EST) refers specifically to the winter offset of UTC-5. Eastern Time (ET) is the general name of the timezone corridor, which encompasses both EST (winter) and Eastern Daylight Time (EDT, UTC-4, summer). Read our guide on <a href="/est-vs-edt/">EST vs EDT</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>EST vs. EDT</strong>: EST is UTC-5, active from early November to mid-March. EDT is UTC-4, active during daylight saving hours from mid-March to early November.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>London vs. New York Difference (5 vs. 4 Hours)</strong>: London and New York are 5 hours apart for approximately 48 weeks of the year. During the 1- to 3-week daylight saving transition mismatch periods in March and October/November, the difference temporarily becomes 4 hours. London and New York are never 6 hours apart.
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

